WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.164
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.464 --> 00:00:09.722
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.022 --> 00:00:15.890
Unit ten: Comparative Law. Today you will contrast legal systems using differ from / contrast with.

4
00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.877
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.833 --> 00:00:22.973
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.817 --> 00:00:25.073
Comparative law.

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:30.742
Comparative law means the study of differences and similarities between legal systems.

8
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:35.010
Comparative law helps reformers learn from other countries.

9
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:38.850
Your turn. Say: Comparative law.

10
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:43.107
Comparative law.

11
00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:45.130
Legal tradition.

12
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:50.566
Legal tradition means a deep-rooted set of attitudes about law and its role.

13
00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.520
Each legal tradition has its own idea of a judge's role.

14
00:00:55.117 --> 00:00:57.897
Your turn. Say: Legal tradition.

15
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:01.955
Legal tradition.

16
00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:04.345
Civil law system.

17
00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:10.473
Civil law system means a system based mainly on comprehensive written codes.

18
00:01:11.083 --> 00:01:14.012
France has a civil law system built on codes.

19
00:01:14.617 --> 00:01:17.780
Your turn. Say: Civil law system.

20
00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:22.772
Civil law system.

21
00:01:23.517 --> 00:01:24.901
Common law system.

22
00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:30.656
Common law system means a system in which judicial decisions create binding precedent.

23
00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:34.144
A common law system develops case by case.

24
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.960
Your turn. Say: Common law system.

25
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.265
Common law system.

26
00:01:43.017 --> 00:01:44.529
Legal transplant.

27
00:01:45.517 --> 00:01:49.699
Legal transplant means a rule borrowed from one legal system into another.

28
00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:52.991
The data-protection rule was a legal transplant.

29
00:01:53.583 --> 00:01:56.793
Your turn. Say: Legal transplant.

30
00:01:59.633 --> 00:02:01.192
Legal transplant.

31
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.172
Harmonization.

32
00:02:04.183 --> 00:02:07.286
Harmonization means making different rules compatible.

33
00:02:07.883 --> 00:02:10.719
Harmonization reduces conflict in trade law.

34
00:02:11.317 --> 00:02:14.004
Your turn. Say: Harmonization.

35
00:02:16.867 --> 00:02:18.309
Harmonization.

36
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.272
Jurisdiction.

37
00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:24.409
Jurisdiction means the authority of a court to hear a case.

38
00:02:25.017 --> 00:02:27.666
The court lacked jurisdiction over the foreign firm.

39
00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.926
Your turn. Say: Jurisdiction.

40
00:02:33.767 --> 00:02:34.954
Jurisdiction.

41
00:02:35.717 --> 00:02:37.403
Cross-border legal issue.

42
00:02:38.417 --> 00:02:42.703
Cross-border legal issue means a legal matter involving more than one country.

43
00:02:43.317 --> 00:02:46.477
A cross-border legal issue complicated the inheritance.

44
00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:51.117
Your turn. Say: Cross-border legal issue.

45
00:02:53.967 --> 00:02:56.152
Cross-border legal issue.

46
00:02:56.967 --> 00:03:00.652
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.517 --> 00:03:06.549
Visal, in my cousin's country the judges don't seem to follow earlier cases. Why?

48
00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:14.830
A civil law system differs from a common law one. Judges apply codes rather than treating precedent as binding.

49
00:03:15.917 --> 00:03:18.172
So their reasoning contrasts with ours?

50
00:03:19.283 --> 00:03:27.497
In emphasis, yes. Common law reasoning contrasts with code-based reasoning because it develops through decided cases.

51
00:03:28.583 --> 00:03:30.745
How can firms operate across both?

52
00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:45.033
Through harmonization and contracts that specify jurisdiction. Each cross-border legal issue differs from the next, so careful drafting matters.

53
00:03:46.167 --> 00:03:47.679
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:48.517 --> 00:03:51.700
Grammar focus: Differ from and contrast with.

55
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:53.292
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:57.109
A civil law system differs from a common law one.

57
00:03:58.017 --> 00:04:01.642
Code-based reasoning contrasts with case-based reasoning.

58
00:04:02.550 --> 00:04:05.873
Each cross-border issue differs from the next in jurisdiction.

59
00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:16.210
differ from / contrast with + B. 'Differ' is a verb, so add -s for he/she/it: differs from. Avoid 'differ than' and 'different than' in formal writing; use 'different from'.

60
00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:20.833
Be careful! Don't say: Civil law differ to common law.

61
00:04:22.767 --> 00:04:25.753
Say: Civil law differs from common law.

62
00:04:26.053 --> 00:04:28.459
differ from (3rd person: differs).

63
00:04:29.267 --> 00:04:31.907
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.131
Two Great Traditions.

65
00:04:34.431 --> 00:04:38.671
Comparative law examines how different societies organise and apply law.

66
00:04:39.667 --> 00:04:48.818
A civil law system, as in France or Cambodia, relies on comprehensive written codes, whereas a common law system, as in England, develops through judicial precedent.

67
00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:55.455
Judges in the civil tradition apply the code; common law judges reason from earlier cases.

68
00:04:56.467 --> 00:05:02.948
Legal transplants, rules borrowed from abroad, often succeed only when adapted to local culture.

69
00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:09.590
Harmonization can reduce friction in trade, though sovereignty and jurisdiction remain sensitive issues.

70
00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:14.725
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:15.583 --> 00:05:18.651
A civil law system blank from a common law one.

72
00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:22.895
A, differs. B, differ. C, differing.

73
00:05:26.433 --> 00:05:28.642
The answer is A: differs.

74
00:05:29.783 --> 00:05:33.106
A rule borrowed from another legal system is a legal blank.

75
00:05:33.406 --> 00:05:37.803
A, tradition. B, transplant. C, harmonization.

76
00:05:41.367 --> 00:05:43.564
The answer is B: transplant.

77
00:05:44.717 --> 00:05:47.424
The authority of a court to hear a case is blank.

78
00:05:47.724 --> 00:05:51.877
A, jurisdiction. B, precedent. C, harmonization.

79
00:05:55.433 --> 00:05:57.746
The answer is A: jurisdiction.

80
00:05:58.883 --> 00:06:00.627
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:00.927 --> 00:06:08.540
A, Civil law differs than common law.. B, Civil law differs from common law.. C, Civil law different from common law..

82
00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.226
The answer is B: Civil law differs from common law..

83
00:06:16.383 --> 00:06:20.716
According to the reading, legal transplants often succeed only when…

84
00:06:21.016 --> 00:06:26.098
A, copied exactly. B, adapted to local culture. C, forced.

85
00:06:29.633 --> 00:06:32.446
The answer is B: adapted to local culture.

86
00:06:33.633 --> 00:06:36.704
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:41.396
What is one difference you know of between legal systems in different countries?

88
00:06:58.183 --> 00:06:59.660
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:24.596
One clear difference concerns precedent. In a common law system, earlier court decisions are binding, so judges create law through cases. In a civil law system, judges apply written codes, and earlier decisions are persuasive but not binding. This difference affects how lawyers argue and how predictable the law feels.

90
00:07:24.896 --> 00:07:26.651
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:31.944
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and differ from and contrast with.

92
00:07:32.244 --> 00:07:38.228
Today's value is global legal awareness. Respect that other societies organise justice differently.

93
00:07:38.528 --> 00:07:41.273
See you in Unit eleven. Goodbye!

94
00:07:41.573 --> 00:07:44.922
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

